  "query_preview": "..\nUnfortunately, the pattern into midweek does not lend itself to\na high confidence forecast given the growing uncertainty \ndetails surrounding the evolution upstream convective activity. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe thunderstorms capable of damaging winds \nand heavy rainfall are possible this afternoon through Tuesday\n\nConvection has been percolating across CPA from late morning \nthrough early afternoon. While much of this activity has been \nfairly shallow and transient so far, a few stronger cores have \nbriefly pulsed to near severe limits before decaying. Weak deep-\nlayer wind shear will continue to modulate the overall storm \nintensity and longevity; however, steady diurnal heating \nthrough broken cloud cover (with smoke aloft) has allowed \ntemperatures to warm into the mid 70s-80s within a relatively \nmoist air mass. This heating/mixing has allowed low-level lapse \nrates to increase to around 8 to 8.5 C/km, which is favorable \nfor isolated downbursts capable of producing strong to damaging\nwind gusts (most likely 40-60 mph). GOES imagery shows several \ndeepening towers and cumulus clusters, hinting that thunderstorm\ncoverage will continue to increase - especially along a subtle \nsurface trough/weak front draped across the region. The \npotential for damaging gusts should increase on the margin in \ntandem with thunderstorm coverage, but the overall threat should\nremain sufficiently localized and transient to preclude watch \nissuance per SWOMCD #1765 (5% probability). Storm motions have \nalso been fairly progressive so far from NNW to SSE which \nshould limit any localized flooding concerns within brief heavy\ndownpours.\n\nTiming and placement uncertainty grows heading into tonight \nwith continued CAM variability concerning downstream \ndevelopment/propagation of Great Lakes/Ohio Valley MCS activity.\nThe main shortwave does appear to reach CPA by Tuesday morning.\nThe decaying? MCS or related feature and associated cloud cover\ncould dampen severe risk into the afternoon. Hires guidance \nseems to suggest an eastward shift in the greatest svr/FF risk \nas the trough deepens over the northern Mid Atlantic. Some heavy\nrain potential will linger into Wednesday as the slow moving \nupper low pivots from northeast PA toward southern New England. \nStorm totals could exceed 2-3 over some parts of the northeast \nCWA by Thursday morning.",
